      "content": "Picture two mountain guides. The first guide swears by a single ancient trail map, insisting every ravine must conform to his favorite theory. The second guide carries multiple sketches, checks changing wind patterns, and tests the snowpack at every ridge.\n\nWhen unexpected blizzards strike, the single-minded guide marches off cliffs. The adaptable scout shifts footing and safely leads the team through shifting terrain.\n\nIn political forecasting, thinkers divide along this exact line. Hedgehogs see the world through a grand organizing idea. They speak with booming certainty and produce tidy narratives.\n\nBut when their predictions collapse, hedgehogs blame bad luck, dishonest opponents, or wrong timing. They refuse to question their core belief. Their fierce devotion to one concept creates massive blind spots.\n\nFoxes work with quiet pragmatism. Instead of relying on a single master theory, they borrow tools from history, economics, psychology, and statistics. They do not fall in love with their own opinions.\n\nWhen fresh evidence emerges, foxes alter their probabilities without shame. They know what they do not know. This quiet intellectual humility makes foxes the best navigators of an erratic future.",
